Daily security audit of the ClaudeOS setup at ${BORG_ROOT}.

Run the /security-review skill against the current state of ${BORG_ROOT}. Specifically review:
- .claude/settings*.json files in each agent's workspace
- hooks configured in those settings
- exposed paths and permission rules
- the consolidated secrets file at ${BORG_ROOT}/.env: must be chmod 600 and
  gitignored (`git check-ignore .env`), and no other filled-in .env / secret
  file may exist elsewhere in the workspace
- LEAKED CREDENTIALS ON DISK — run `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/credential-sweep.sh` and
  report on its exit code. Do NOT hand-roll this sweep or substitute an ad-hoc
  `grep`; the script exists precisely because improvising it failed. On
  2026-08-23 four consecutive audits reported "zero live token values on disk"
  while 25 files under Xcode's default DerivedData held live credentials — the
  swept paths had been carried in each run's working memory and had quietly
  drifted to the wrong directory. The script discovers cache roots at run time,
  pins /usr/bin/grep, matches the real values read from the secret stores rather
  than provider prefixes, and self-tests before trusting a clean result.
  Interpret its exit code literally:
    0 = clean. Report the root count it swept, so scope drift is visible in the
        report rather than invisible.
    1 = credentials found. CRITICAL. It prints variable name, file path, and
        mode; any file marked WORLD-READABLE is the most urgent. Report the
        exposing variable NAME and never the value — this report is emailed.
    2 = the sweep could not be trusted (canary missed, empty pattern set, or a
        grep that errored). This is NOT a clean result and must never be
        reported as one. Escalate as a finding in its own right: the audit was
        blind for this run.
  If a credential is found, also check whether it reached anywhere durable
  before recommending rotation urgency: pickaxe AND all-object scan of every
  repo's history (reachable plus unreachable/dangling objects, which catches
  committed-then-rewritten), and whether the path sits under the Google Drive
  mirror root (read the mirror's own roots; do not assume the mirror's scope).
- any new MCP servers added since last audit
- `${BORG_ROOT}/tmp/notify-failures.log` — the sentinel `run-scheduled-task.sh`
  writes when `notify-email.sh` fails. Email is the only outbound channel, so
  entries here are notifications the user never received (including failure
  alerts). Report any entry newer than the last audit as a finding, then note
  it was surfaced. Absent or empty file = nothing to report.
- the independent repos under ${BORG_ROOT}/repos/ (git-ignored — enumerate the
  filesystem, not git status; e.g. repos/waiq): each repo's .claude/settings*.json
  and the hooks those settings configure, its .claude/commands/ and skills for
  anything that executes or exfiltrates, any .mcp.json or MCP server not
  registered in ${BORG_ROOT}/c4po/MCP.md, and a secret-pattern sweep of the
  repo's config files. The workspace-wide credential sweep above already covers
  these repos by value; this pass is the complementary one — unknown-shaped
  secrets in config that no env file would list. Triage keyword matches
  individually before reporting: dictionaries and text corpora legitimately
  contain the words "secret" and "token".
- Before classifying repeats, read the prior state file. Preserve any
  `accepted_risks` entries and treat them as resolved, not findings, unless the
  current facts materially exceed the scope the user accepted. Carry the
  entries forward when writing the new state.

Always email the result by piping it into `${BORG_ROOT}/.bin/notify-email.sh c4po "Borg security audit — {YYYY-MM-DD} — {N} finding(s)"` (reads the message from stdin and emails it to the user) — even when the audit is clean, so the user can confirm the job ran. Body header: same as the subject.
- If anything critical is found (auth weakness, dangerous permission, broken access policy, suspicious MCP server, leaked secret), include each finding and a recommended fix.
- If clean, the body is a one-liner: `All clear — no findings.`

Tooling constraints that have silently voided a run before — treat as hard rules:
- Pin every sweep to `/usr/bin/grep`. The interactive `grep` here is a ugrep shim
  that honours `--ignore-files` and can skip gitignored paths, which is exactly
  where secrets live.
- `timeout` does not exist on macOS. `timeout N grep ... | head` exits 127 and
  prints nothing, which is indistinguishable from a clean result. Never pipe a
  sweep through `head` either: the pipeline reports head's status, not grep's.
  Redirect to a file and inspect grep's own exit code.
- A full credential sweep takes several minutes across all roots. Let it finish;
  a truncated sweep that reports clean is worse than no sweep.
